{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 37, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a powerful figure in the 12th century known for his military prowess and political acumen. He ruled the County of Tripoli from 1146 to 1199 leaving a significant mark on the region's history. His reign was marked by both expansionist ambitions and internal challenges.\n\n*Early Life and Ascension* Raymond was the son of Raymond I Count of Tripoli. He inherited the county following his father's death in 1146. His early reign saw him consolidating control over the county's territories and strengthening its defenses. He faced challenges from neighboring powers including the Byzantine Empire and the Holy Roman Empire. Raymond skillfully navigated these conflicts often employing diplomacy and military force to protect Tripoli's interests. He established a strong military force which proved crucial in defending the county against various threats.\n\nRaymond III's reign was characterized by significant military campaigns. He led expeditions against Muslim forces in Syria and Palestine expanding Tripoli's territory. He also engaged in conflicts with the Byzantine Empire over control of Cyprus. His military successes enhanced Tripoli's prestige and solidified its position as a major power in the Levant. He maintained a relatively large army often employing mercenaries to bolster his forces.\n\n*Political and Economic Policies* Raymond III was a shrewd administrator. He focused on improving the county's economy through trade and agriculture. He fostered relations with merchants and encouraged economic development within Tripoli. He also implemented policies to improve the lives of his subjects. His governance was often characterized by a blend of feudal traditions and more modern administrative practices for the time. He built infrastructure improved justice and maintained a degree of stability within his domain.\n\nRaymond III's reign also saw a period of cultural flourishing in Tripoli. The county became a center of learning and art attracting scholars and artists from across the Mediterranean. He promoted the arts and sciences fostering a vibrant intellectual environment. He was a patron of the arts and supported various religious institutions.\n\n*Legacy and Death* Raymond III Count of Tripoli died in 1199. His reign was considered a golden age for the county. He left behind a stable and prosperous kingdom with a strong military and a thriving economy. His military campaigns expanded Tripoli's territory and solidified its position as a regional power. He is remembered as a successful ruler who defended Tripoli's interests and fostered its cultural development. His legacy continues to be studied by historians who view him as one of the most important rulers of the Levant during the 12th century. His accomplishments in both military and political spheres ensured Tripoli’s survival and prosperity for many years.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.1804991215467453}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": -1.6689286894688848e-06}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -6.115249561844394e-05}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -2.861018856492592e-06}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁was", "logprob": -0.08474750816822052}, {"token": "▁a", "logprob": -0.03216259181499481}, {"token": "▁powerful", "logprob": -0.5741356611251831}, {"token": "▁figure", "logprob": -1.5933756828308105}, {"token": "▁in", "logprob": -0.023135026916861534}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.003552081063389778}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -0.7682257294654846}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.0013236580416560173}, {"token": "th", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁century", "logprob": -0.041833218187093735}, {"token": "▁known", "logprob": -1.4091229438781738}, {"token": "▁for", "logprob": -0.01041017472743988}, {"token": "▁his", "logprob": -0.0042852009646594524}, {"